    "note": [
        {
            "structuredValue": [],
            "parallelValue": [],
            "groupedValue": [],
            "value": "Pain sensation, also known as nociception, involves a complex system of neural connections that balance inhibition and excitation. Although the mechanisms that determine the level of nociception at the peripheral nervous system have been extensively studied, similar pathways in the central nervous system are not as well defined. Moreover, our understanding of how sustained inflammation, often found in chronic pain diseases, affects these pathways is not complete. Descending control circuits from the brainstem to the spinal cord are strong candidates for the modulation of nociception. The locus coeruleus (LC) is a nucleus in the pons of the brain stem that broadly innervates cortical areas involved in arousal, cognition, and memory. The LC descends down to the spinal cord and has been suggested to play an important role in modulating pain transmission in the spinal cord. In this study, we explored the possible influence of the noradrenergic descending projections from the LC to the dorsal horn on pain sensation. Combining a novel retrograde tracing strategy with Dbh-Cre mice, we achieved specific targeting of the spinal cord projecting neurons in the LC (LC→SC). This approach enabled us to use targeted chemogenetic manipulation to activate the LC→SC neurons and test the effect of this descending pathway on pain sensation. Our preliminary results indicated that activation of the LC→SC neurons decreased pain sensitivity, revealing an inhibitory role in nociception. We also found that LC inhibition of pain decreases in intensity in the presence of chronic inflammation, suggesting a possible connection between LC activity and chronic pain. Moreover, we employed a novel retrograde tracing method, named cTRIO, to identify brain wide monosynaptic inputs onto the LC→SC neurons. Together, results of this study advanced our understanding of the role of the locus coeruleus in nociception and may provide insights for developing novel pain treating strategies that do not have the addictive characteristics of currently used opiates.",
